SM(213,{"n":"1 Timothy 3.1-13","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Timothy 3.1-13.mp3","d":2908.9,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.16,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.72,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[21.84,"When we get started in just a moment, we'll be picking up in St."],[26.32,"Paul's first epistle to Timothy chapter 3, verse 1."],[33.76,"And so we'll do a brief catch-up introduction."],[40.08,"We covered the completely non-controversial part last time."],[43.84,"So uh this is gonna be less controversial, therefore perhaps less exciting from here on out."],[51.44,"Um I don't know, I say controversial in the first."],[54.0,"Well, yeah, chat yeah, chapter three is slightly, but not as controversial as the I command women to remain silent."],[59.92,"That that that pretty much takes the cake for uh controversial in uh First Timothy"],[67.4,"Um so this uh as we've as we've mentioned now, um this is the first of several epistles, several letters from St."],[77.48,"Paul that we're gonna be reading that are now that are directed towards individuals rather than communities."],[84.92,"Um and uh the ones we were previous to this were either directed to a church like the Christian community in Corinth, or they were directed to the Christian communities of a region like Galatia or uh St."],[100.2,"Paul's Epistle to the Ephesians, that was sort of a circular letter for the area around Ephesus, whereas this one obviously is written directly to St."],[104.84,"Timothy And we've already seen how Saint Paul has frequently used this sort of father-son language to describe his relationship to St."],[122.0,"Timothy. This and 2 Timothy and Titus are often called the pastoral. there we should really get the idea of sort of mentorship"],[139.12,"That's the idea."],[140.08,"The idea is that Saint Paul is mentoring and preparing St."],[145.92,"Timothy for this leadership role in the churches."],[149.84,"We talked about how St"],[151.56,"Paul is writing this very near the end of his life and during what is likely his second uh Roman imprisonment."],[160.92,"And so he knows that he is uh nearing the end of his life."],[165.88,"And so uh a big part of the subtext underlying this letter and 2 Timothy is the idea that uh St."],[176.44,"Paul has, of course, been traveling with St."],[178.44,"Timothy, mentoring him."],[179.8,"They've had this relationship."],[181.08,"St. Timothy has sort of served as his messenger and envoy, has been sent to different communities when St."],[187.16,"Paul couldn't go himself."],[189.4,"Um, and now he's sort of giving St."],[194.28,"Timothy his final instructions to really send him out, sort of in St."],[194.84,"Paul's place, because St"],[200.88,"Paul knows he's he's about to depart."],[204.24,"And um it's going to be important for what we're going to read this evening."],[208.64,"Um that we understand that this is this is a transition that takes place at the beginning of the last third of the first century."],[219.68,"So around 6667, this is when Saint Paul and St."],[224.24,"Peter are martyred."],[226.08,"Uh there were apostles who were martyred before them."],[229.36,"The rest of the apostles, other than Saint John, are martyred after them."],[234.74,"But all within the end of the first century."],[237.94,"And so now we're in this period where there's a transition from the apostles themselves, the direct witnesses who knew Christ and were his disciples during his earthly ministry and observed all these events to now their disciples."],[259.66,"So the transition from first generation to second generation is happening."],[265.42,"And this is part of the reason why what are called the pastoral epistles read a little differently."],[272.54,"We talked in the in the introduction about how some people, because they read a little differently, either want to say St."],[277.82,"Paul didn't write them, or you know, etc."],[280.46,"etc. But the reason part of the reason they read a little differently is that there's there's a transition in the type of New Testament literature that we get."],[289.32,"So the Gospels were written after St."],[293.32,"Paul's epistles. We've talked about that before."],[296.36,"St. Paul's epistles were the first, were among the first things written."],[300.81,"And those first things that St."],[303.61,"Paul wrote were written to these directly to these communities."],[309.61,"We've seen how they were about very practical things going on in those communities."],[313.77,"They're him continuing his pastoral role in the communities that he helped found, or in the case of like Romans, exercising a pastoral role in a community he didn't found, but in which he had a recognized authority to help them with their practical issues."],[332.29,"But now, once the apostles themselves and the eyewitnesses begin to leave this world and this life, now there's a need for them to start putting in writing their testimony, their wisdom, their guidance for the next generation."],[352.77,"Because they aren't going to be there in person to say that anymore."],[355.73,"And this is what the fathers say."],[360.85,"This is why Saint Mark writes his gospel."],[363.73,"Mark goes to Alexandria in Egypt and he writes down what St"],[363.95,"St. Peter is martyred."],[365.63,"St. Peter had told him."],[371.27,"He takes St."],[371.83,"Peter's testimony about Christ and writes it down."],[373.11,"He can't travel and preach and tell about these things anymore"],[375.15,"Because Saint Peter's gone now."],[380.67,"People hear it has to be written down."],[382.91,"And then similar thing with St."],[385.87,"Matthew's Gospel. Saint Luke, when he writes his gospel, says, Hey, I went and found a bunch of right, a bunch of people have written things."],[394.03,"I pulled them all together and talked to people and writes his gospel."],[398.59,"And then as we talked about back when we were going through Saint John's Gospel, he seems to have been familiar with all the other ones."],[406.03,"And in places, seems to be deliberately filling in gaps."],[410.35,"Like deliberately, having seen the other gospels and maybe some others we don't even have anymore, says, Oh, hey, here's another story, or here's another piece, or here's something else Christ said that I think is important that wasn't recorded in these others."],[426.45,"Um, but so that that accounts for this shift in St."],[430.77,"Paul's writing, too."],[432.55,"Because this is why St."],[434.07,"Paul now, here at the end of his life, stops writing to communities to deal with the day-to-day stuff, starts writing to people who are going to be the people who are going to be dealing with that day-to-day stuff."],[446.79,"You know, going into the next generation."],[449.67,"Um the next generation is going to be have a living memory of the apostles."],[456.17,"So the first generation of apostles has a living memory of Christ Himself."],[460.89,"Third generation has a sort of a living memory of them"],[461.41,"Second generation has a living memory of the apostles."],[467.62,"By the time you get to the end of the second century, you don't really have that living memory anymore."],[474.66,"And so there's another transition there when you're reading the Fathers at the end of the second and beginning of the third century."],[482.5,"There's another sort of transition there where you're now outside living memory, and so it's about trusting what has come before."],[490.82,"And this is what then develops into the whole process of tradition, within the church."],[497.32,"But so uh St"],[500.36,"Paul very much has this in mind as he's writing to St."],[503.16,"Timothy. It's not just that he's preparing St."],[505.4,"Timothy to take this role, he's preparing him to take this role knowing that he's not going to be there in the flesh, to uh have that role himself."],[516.85,"Um that's very much the case both in 1 Timothy and when we eventually get to 2 Timothy, it's the case there too."],[525.41,"Uh so all that said, unless there are any questions or comments or timely reminders, uh, we'll go ahead and pick up in St."],[535.89,"Paul's first epistle to Timothy chapter 3, verse 1."],[543.49,"This is a faithful saying."],[546.73,"If a man desires the position of a bishop, he desires a good work."],[553.53,"So first we'll comment on the translation bishop, because this is already something that people will have an issue with."],[563.21,"So the word there, episcopos, is the word that's pretty much always been translated bishop in English."],[570.47,"So we could just leave it in Greek if we wanted to, episcopos."],[575.59,"As uh this office."],[577.35,"It literally means in Greek uh someone who watches over."],[582.95,"And so a lot of times it's translated very literally into English as overseer."],[589.29,"Which is super literal."],[591.38,"The scopos and episcopos is like telescope, you know, microscope, scope, to see, to look."],[600.22,"But that word has behind it a Hebrew word from the Old Testament."],[610.06,"It's basically Ra'im."],[611.66,"Ra'a means to see, to look or to see."],[615.34,"And that word in its noun form referred to a herdsman or a shepherd."],[624.54,"Someone who watches over flocks, someone who watches over herds."],[630.66,"Um, so this is an adaptation, in Greek."],[635.88,"It's not St."],[636.76,"Paul's inv'ents inving aenting a new term, he new office."],[640.88,"He's like, here's how I think we should structure the church."],[644.0,"Tell me what you think."],[645.38,"It's it's picking up on this theme."],[648.12,"We see the leaders of Israel all through the Old Testament are called the shepherds of Israel."],[654.38,"Whether they're getting chewed out or whether they're being praised, they're called the the shepherds of Israel."],[658.7,"They weren't always good shepherds, but uh they were they were to be shepherds."],[663.18,"And so this is the same language."],[664.7,"That's why this gets translated into Latin as pastor, which means shepherd."],[671.98,"So they were aware of the root behind Episcopal."],[676.22,"So that''ss it a little lost in the English word bishop."],[679.19,"It's not sort of what's the etymology of bishop?"],[681.19,"I do not know"],[683.87,"That's uh if Father Andrew listens to this, he can Facebook message me and tell me."],[688.99,"He's the he's studying Old English right now, so he could probably uh let me know."],[694.27,"But I am not an English language scholar."],[696.99,"With with the Episcopus having this shepherd like me, is that the word for shepherd in in Greek at this time, or would they have a no."],[707.07,"No, okay. No, that is not the normal word for shepherd in Greek."],[710.27,"Okay. Um, but it's the word in Greek that's used to translate that word for herdsman in the Old Testament."],[717.15,"Yeah. Yeah. It doesn't have that other kind."],[720.3,"There are other words specifically for shepherd in in Greek."],[723.4,"But so yeah, so say."],[724.88,"Paul is taking this word not just out of the Greek language, but very deliberately from the Greek of the Old Testament to refer to this role in the Old Testament."],[738.56,"This is this is important for a whole bunch of reasons."],[742.56,"But the biggest one is as I've harp as I harp on all the time, we we tend to have this idea as sort of modern Western Christians that Christ comes and then the Holy Spirit comes, and it's like, okay, we're starting from scratch, new religion time."],[759.78,"Uh, and that's not how any of the apostles saw themselves."],[763.42,"They saw themselves as continuing the same religion they had before."],[768.3,"It's just now the Messiah's come, which we were expecting to happen, and the Holy Spirit has come, which Joel prophesied would happen."],[775.43,"So this is all part and parcel."],[777.51,"And so when the apostles are structuring the church."],[783.45,"I mean, they were leading the church while they're alive, but as they come to the end of their lives, as they're like structuring the church going forward, they're not inventing some new structure."],[792.49,"They're not taking something, as one of my old professors in Protestant seminary used to say, they're not sort of borrowing the magisterial bishop from Roman civil government somehow."],[805.1,"Like uh they're thinking like a governor or something."],[809.34,"But they're they're directly continuing, and so these terms, whether it's episcopos, whether it's presbyter, which gets translated elder in the old testament."],[820.9,"70 elders, the elders of Israel, the Sanhedrin, were presbyters in Greek."],[826.75,"All of the structures of Israel in the Old Testament remain as structures of Israel in the New Testament, meaning the church."],[837.87,"So there's a there's a transition, obviously, because Christ fulfills these things, but they aren't sort of scrapped and some new thing comes and takes their place."],[846.73,"They're fulfilled and developed."],[848.97,"Paul is talking about that"],[849.81,"So the way St."],[855.13,"So a lot of people argued, we talked about this in the introduction, that to this book that a lot of people, this is one of those things that they'll argue, oh, well, St."],[863.49,"Paul can't have written this because there can't have been bishops yet while St."],[869.89,"Paul was alive because they were starting from scratch and it took time for those structures to develop."],[876.77,"But if you understand that these are structures that already existed."],[881.35,"So they're just they're they're putting these into place in a Christian context."],[888.07,"So we saw in the book of Acts."],[890.01,"There were presbyters, there were elders of the community in Ephesus."],[895.69,"This isn't something St."],[896.97,"Paul invented in Ephesus."],[898.57,"This is there was a Sanhedrin back in Jerusalem."],[901.57,"There were elders back in Jerusalem who were in charge of the religious community."],[908.29,"So it's just he's applying the same structure."],[911.41,"Now these are Christian presbyters, Christian elders, Christian overseers, Christian shepherds, Christian pastors."],[920.37,"Distinctly, but it's not any kind of radical departure."],[925.01,"So there's no reason why St."],[926.29,"Paul couldn't have written this."],[928.93,"And all he says here is that this is an established."],[931.69,"He who desires to be a pastor, he who desires this position of leadership of the church, the leadership of a particular Christian community, desires a good work."],[943.25,"Now notice he doesn't say that's just a good desire no matter why they want it."],[949.37,"But doing that, doing that faithfully is a good work."],[954.25,"He's already talked about good works, remember, earlier."],[957.63,"Being zealous of good work."],[958.99,"This is one way in which one can do that is by having this position of leadership in your community."],[966.75,"But you then have to be about doing the good work, if that's if that's what you want."],[972.11,"It's not just about the authority."],[976.91,"Verse 2. A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife, temperate, sober-minded, of good behavior, hospitable, able to. teach"],[990.47,"Okay, so he's talking about this is the type of person, who should have this role."],[997.8,"Why would he be telling St."],[999.24,"Timothy this? Because St."],[1002.36,"Timothy is going to be the one who is helping appoint bishops, in over these these churches."],[1011.7,"So he's saying these are the kind of people who you need to to put into this office."],[1018.94,"Um it's maybe just because I'm a Catechan and I haven't learned everything."],[1024.46,"No one here has learned everything, so you're okay."],[1027.34,"Well, I understand the bishops these days though, they have to be celibate and even monastic, I think."],[1032.14,"So well, sort of."],[1035.42,"That's yeah. So yeah, we'll get to that in a second."],[1038.7,"First the blameless part, then we'll get to the husband of one way."],[1041.98,"So um blameless, does not mean they have never sinned in their life, obviously, because otherwise we wouldn't have any bishops."],[1053.6,"Uh the the word that's used here for blameless is referring to uh blameless as a real literal translation."],[1060.48,"It refers to public guilt or shame or scandal kind of thing."],[1066.08,"It's more like they need to be of good reputation."],[1069.22,"This can't be someone who's publicly, you know, seemed to be problematic."],[1075.54,"Yes. And then the husband of one wife, what it literally says in the Greek is he has to be a one-woman man."],[1087.48,"And so um this is not meant to imply that he has to be married."],[1095.56,"This is meant to imply that he can't have been married more than once."],[1103.16,"Either because he was widowed and then remarried, or divorced and remarried, or polygamous."],[1116.4,"And uh yeah, we people are generally not aware that. major Jewish communities practiced polygamy until the 10th century AD"],[1128.4,"Uh so there that yeah, so there were situations."],[1133.12,"Um this is saying uh none of that."],[1138.28,"Um now, in terms of modern practice, we talked about this a little when we when we read about um St."],[1145.88,"Paul talking about St."],[1146.84,"Peter's wife and kids."],[1148.92,"Um and uh but so that the current practice in the Orthodox Church is that because of the nature of the episcopate currently, uh at this time, and in the early church, a bishop was the bishop of a city."],[1166.44,"And that was it"],[1172.64,"He presided at that one church in that one city."],[1175.86,"So he was in one place."],[1179.06,"That eventually developed until there were, as Christianity grew."],[1183.56,"You'd have a place like Antioch and there would be seven or eight churches in the city."],[1187.88,"And then the little villages surrounding that city would each have their own church."],[1191.16,"And then, so now the bishop is over a whole bunch of churches."],[1195.1,"And so he would send his presbyters out to preside at those at the churches he wasn't at."],[1202.6,"On that, on a given uh for a given uh liturgy."],[1207.08,"Um and now in our current practice, that's grown to these huge geographical areas sometime."],[1210.04,"Well in the US it has"],[1213.6,"In the old country, in a lot of places it's still a relatively small, the various old countries, but um like there are hundreds of bishops in Russia."],[1222.16,"I mean Russia is a big place, but there's hundreds of bishops over little little bits of territory."],[1228.56,"Um but so you can look at the US as an example."],[1233.09,"Arbishop, Bishop Nicholas is headquartered in Miami, Florida, and covers everything from North Carolina to Memphis, Tennessee, down to here in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[1246.47,"And before COVID, as you guys know who have been here, he's traveling every weekend."],[1252.21,"He was in a different place."],[1254.81,"So that kind of lifestyle is not conducive to having a wife and children."],[1263.34,"So um it has generally been the practice, and I say generally the practice, because there are tons of exceptions to this, but it's generally been the practice that for the last several centuries, that only uh unmarried priests are, well, unmarried people are elevated to the episcopate."],[1288.22,"Um the best source of unmarried people, obviously, who would be good candidates are monastics."],[1299.46,"But that is a practice."],[1302.58,"There have been exceptions to it."],[1305.14,"And that's also not because there's something wrong with being married."],[1310.34,"This is something we have to be very clear about."],[1313.7,"Because it has been very common in those same centuries for men who for priests who were widowed to be consecrated as bishops."],[1331.34,"So in our own archdiocese, Bishop John was married and has children."],[1339.14,"But uh his wife passed away."],[1342.18,"His children are all adults and have their own households"],[1347.82,"And so he no longer had any concerns in that regard that would stop him from being consecrated as a bishop."],[1356.46,"And so he's now a bishop."],[1358.7,"So it's uh the not being married part is a practical consideration."],[1364.22,"Um and it's actually based on that passage we read earlier from St."],[1367.58,"Paul where he talks about, you know, don't I have the right if I wanted to to bring a wife and children with me, as St."],[1375.1,"Peter does. But St."],[1377.08,"Paul says, I don',t because he thinks it's better to because he's an itinerant evangelist and getting thrown in prison and shipwrecked and all these things, not to do that."],[1387.33,"So it's a practical thing and a question of calling."],[1391.81,"But St. Paul is emphasizing here, you could only have been married that once."],[1397.41,"Tops. And so temperate, sober-minded, good behavior, spitable, able to teach as good, since that's a big part of their role."],[1412.85,"Continuing in verse 3, not given to wine."],[1415.97,"So I think we all know what that means."],[1418.61,"Not violent, um not greedy for money, but gentle, not quarrelsome, not covetous, uh, one who rules his own house well, having his children in submission with all reverence."],[1433.69,"For if a man does not know how to rule his own house, how will he take care of the church of God?"],[1440.33,"So this is an extension of something we've talked about."],[1443.93,"In the Old Testament, the concept of priesthood and fatherhood and even masculinity in general are connected at the most basic level."],[1455.29,"Um, so in the Old Testament, what it is to be a man essentially is to be a priest."],[1462.27,"Within your within your household."],[1465.47,"And so St."],[1466.35,"Paul is just sort of applying that."],[1468.77,"It's like this this if this man's married and has children, then his first priesthood is to his family."],[1475.41,"Meaninging he should be representing God to them and them to God in prayer, lead them in worship."],[1475.97,"he And if can't do that well and successfully in his own household, if there are problems going on in his old house, not just like it's a moral failing, but he needs to be paying attention."],[1493.85,"If if his children are having problems, he needs to be attending to that, not going and being a bishop."],[1501.14,"He needs to be focused on that first."],[1503.94,"If all of that is functioning and all of that is in line and all of that is going well, then he can take on more, he's able to take on more responsibility within the household of God."],[1516.12,"Over the sort of the larger family of the community."],[1519.08,"But if that's not okay, he needs his focus needs to be there"],[1525.5,"Not trying to take on more."],[1528.86,"And um, yeah."],[1531.66,"St. Paul is giving really good advice here that all too often uh we we priests don't take."],[1540.46,"Uh a lot of priests struggle about balancing sort of our own family and the community, and having to choose where do I devote my time and my energy."],[1555.26,"And a lot of priests' families have suffered."],[1559.78,"Um things have not gone well because we we. don't heed, don't heed St"],[1566.82,"Paul's advice here."],[1574.18,"Verse 6, not a novice, lest being puffed up with pride, he fall into the same condemnation as the devil."],[1582.42,"So, novice meaning he's not new."],[1585.32,"He didn't just become a Christian last week."],[1588.06,"But he seems smart and he talks good, so let's make him the bishop."],[1591.54,"Uh not a good plan."],[1593.72,"But notice why St."],[1594.84,"Paul says it's not a good plan."],[1598.44,"It's not like, oh, he's gonna do damage in the church or he's gonna teach heresy or he' goings to be doing correct stuff."],[1604.28,"It's not gonna be good for him."],[1608.58,"It's not going to be good for him for you to put him in that position."],[1614.1,"Because he's going to get proud."],[1615.94,"Zit Kagi, remember how that worked out for the devil."],[1619.54,"The whole being proud thing."],[1624.5,"So this is another place where you get that mentorship from St."],[1628.5,"Paul. That mentorship from St."],[1631.48,"Paul. That there's a concern."],[1638.73,"For the people who are going to be in these positions of leadership."],[1644.98,"If them being in that position of leadership, if this person being a bishop, he could be seem like on paper the guy who would be the best bishop ever."],[1650.26,"But if it's going to be bad for his family, then no, don't do it"],[1656.94,"If it's going to be bad for his salvation, don't do it."],[1660.94,"Doesn't matter how qualified he is."],[1662.86,"Doesn't matter how much people like him."],[1666.0,"These things are more important."],[1671.68,"Verse 7. Moreover, he must have a good testimony among those who are outside, lest he fall into reproach in the snare of the devil."],[1680.32,"Those who are outside the Christian community."],[1684.08,"So it's not just everybody in the Christian community thinks he's great."],[1688.54,"But the people, why?"],[1689.82,"Because St. Paul's concerned about getting those people outside to come inside."],[1694.74,"And if having this person as your leader is gonna be not conducive to that, that's gonna be an obstacle to the gospel, bringing those people into the community, then again, not the right person."],[1709.14,"I have a question."],[1714.34,"We have lots of stories of saints who were bishops, who usually or very often the story tells about how he did everything he could to avoid being made bishop."],[1727.46,"Yes. Um and yet at the beginning it says wanting to be bishop is a good thing."],[1734.5,"No, he doesn't say wanting to be a bishop is a good thing."],[1737.54,"Okay. He says being a bishop is a good work."],[1739.46,"Yeah Okay. That's an important distinction."],[1746.18,"Because if you're wanting the authority or the power, that's not good."],[1752.79,"If you're wanting to serve God, and do the work, then that's a good desire to have."],[1762.35,"But right after St."],[1764.35,"Paul says that, he gives a whole list of things why you might say to that person, hey, I know you're wanting to do a good work, but this isn't the right thing."],[1779.01,"We just got a whole little checklist."],[1781.51,"Of like, maybe not, maybe not, maybe not, maybe not, maybe not."],[1786.31,"Yeah. So yeah, St."],[1788.79,"Augustine, they literally drug him to the front of the church in tears to make him a bishop."],[1793.67,"But they all thought he was crying because he was so honored and touched and having this spiritual experience."],[1797.99,"And he's like, No, I was terrified."],[1801.51,"He did not want it."],[1803.27,"Um, so yeah, there is something to be said for that, but that's not always true, too."],[1810.31,"Like, sometimes somebody doesn't want to be a bishop, and they're right."],[1814.63,"You know? L likeike they know themselves well enough to know that that's not for them."],[1820.92,"And sometimes someone is the perfect person, and part of the reason they're the perfect person is that they don't want it."],[1824.92,"They don't want the power, they don't want the, you know, um, the authority and stuff"],[1831.98,"So, yeah, it could go either way."],[1833.98,"Both of those could go either way."],[1840.03,"Okay. Verse 8."],[1843.55,"Likewise, deacons must be reverent, not double-tongued, not given to much wine, not greedy for money, holding the mystery of the faith with a pure conscience."],[1854.59,"Okay. So, likewise means, same goes for for deacons."],[1863.13,"And I think we talked before about how deacons are roughly parallel to the uh back in Acts, I think we did, roughly parallel to like the Levites in the in the Old Testament."],[1874.07,"You had the priesthood, and then you had the Levites, the other Levites who were not strictly speaking in the priestly family, who assisted and did all of these other sub ministries."],[1884.24,"And that's what we're talking about with deacon."],[1885.84,"The word deacon is just from the word servant."],[1893.68,"This is what a continuation of the household of God idea."],[1896.8,"There's sort of servants in God's household."],[1901.48,"The question about that that haunts me a lot of the time comes up haunts because you're all but uh the picture I got from Acts as it was interpreted in the Protestant world that I grew up in was that the deacons took care of money matters, uh food, practical things like that."],[1926.2,"Our deacons are mainly liturgical assistants."],[1933.08,"How did if that was true of the early deacons, and if it was not, just straighten me out on that?"],[1940.6,"How did that transition happen?"],[1943.96,"Well, that's the liturgical role of our deacons."],[1947.4,"But our deacons actually have a larger ministry than that."],[1952.36,"Okay. Uh, or should."],[1955.3,"Um deacons are actually the ones responsible and have been throughout the church's history for bringing communion to the sick, to making sure that widows, orphans, people in need are taken care of."],[1972.76,"They sort of had that external pastoral role."],[1979.64,"We haven't had yet, our bishops are working on it, but in the United States, we haven't had a very lively permanent diaconate historically."],[1990.36,"That's to our detriment"],[1993.56,"But the typically the the presbyter would be involved in sort of the internal ministry of the church."],[2001.48,"Presiding in, yeah, yeah, that episcopal role"],[2003.84,"He's a substitute."],[2008.3,"And it's the deacon who had sort of the ministry of mercy to the broader community."],[2017.34,"So that's more historically."],[2019.52,"And the deacons also have a liturgical role, just like presbyters have a liturgical role."],[2024.88,"And could be because bishops have a liturgical role. I should say"],[2032.8,"Bishops and deacons do."],[2034.48,"And presbyters do the parts that whoever isn't there uh would do most of the parts."],[2042.8,"So, like if there's a bishop and a deacon at a liturgy. the the priests just sort of stand there"],[2049.64,"The bishop he's usually nice and throws us a bone you know has us do a couple of prayers here and there but he doesn't have to they're not really our parts that's he's just sort of being nice so we don't fall asleep."],[2060.6,"Um yeah but yeah so that that that's the liturgical role but that's not that isn't supposed to be the whole role of the deacon any more than doing the services should be the whole role of the priest in the community."],[2077.08,"Um yeah that helps uh so he says, you know, likewise, so all that goes for deacons, he reiterates some of it."],[2091.44,"Verse 10, but let these also first be tested, then let them serve as deacons, being blameless."],[2100.33,"So that blameless is it's the same word, but it's referring to the testing."],[2106.23,"Meaning you don't just like, oh, this is a nice guy, he seems like a good kid, let's make him a deacon."],[2112.23,"He cares about people."],[2114.07,"Um, but they also have to be."],[2117.37,"And for the same reason."],[2119.31,"Same policies. Not only if you do this with the wrong person, is this going to be bad for the community, but it's going to be bad for the person and for the person's family and for everybody involved."],[2129.05,"So there's testing first."],[2131.53,"Okay. And now we get to what will probably be the actual uh controversial part of tonight's Bible study."],[2141.17,"Uh because verse 11 is translated completely wrong."],[2144.61,"Oh. Um you notice there's some italicized words there."],[2155.65,"Uh and there's also the word wives."],[2159.09,"That's not what it says."],[2161.97,"It says women."],[2164.21,"Likewise, women. There's no their, and there's no wives."],[2172.05,"Likewise, women, reverent, not slanderers, temperate, faithful in all things."],[2178.45,"Because it's talking about deaconesses"],[2180.97,"Okay? Why is this important?"],[2188.57,"When you look at early church orders like the Dida Ke, deaconesses are not women who are deacons."],[2198.17,"Deaconess is a separate office."],[2200.89,"It is a separate thing."],[2204.01,"That's why you notice St."],[2206.65,"Paul introduces it with another likewise."],[2209.37,"He says, likewise deacons, likewise women."],[2213.22,"So when he shifts from one office to the next, he's using that word likewise."],[2219.46,"But the word he uses there for women is not the word for a wife."],[2223.9,"That's why I say it's mistranslated."],[2227.02,"It's uh yineki as opposed to uh yine."],[2234.78,"Yine is a woman or a wife, like a woman."],[2239.02,"In the same way that there's a Greek word anir that means a man or a husband."],[2244.22,"There's also anthropos that means man like humanity, like mankind."],[2250.88,"When you have ineki, that means woman."],[2254.9,"Like womanhood, womankind."],[2258.39,"So this isn't the word for a woman or a wife."],[2263.89,"That's part of how we know both that he's not talking about wives and that he's not talking about just women who are deacons."],[2270.66,"Because if it was just women who are deacons, it would have been the other word."],[2276.5,"But in the early church orders like the Didache, when they talk about deaconesses, there's not a word that's translated deaconess."],[2284.74,"It's literally deacon women."],[2291.7,"So there's deacons, and when you read those early church orders like the D Daka K and uh the Apostolic Constitutions, they have here's what deacons do, here's what deacon women do."],[2309.62,"Different things. Because this is a different office"],[2312.2,"So this is the ancient office of deaconess, which still technically exists in the Orthodox Church."],[2318.2,"It's just not used very much."],[2321.16,"Some abbesses of convents are actually deaconesses."],[2326.28,"They've actually been made deaconesses by the bishop."],[2329.16,"But that's about it in today's Orthodox world."],[2332.6,"It's just fallen into disuse."],[2338.04,"So there's might as well."],[2341.72,"It's gonna be controversial anyway."],[2343.48,"So there's a lot of dialogue about this right now in the Orthodox Church."],[2349.24,"Why is there dialogue about it?"],[2351.0,"Well, on one hand, the reason they had deaconesses was that there were things culturally that it was inappropriate for men to do."],[2362.58,"So at a certain point in church history, people were baptized naked."],[2366.42,"Going down to the water with a naked woman is not something that male deacons were supposed to do."],[2372.28,"So women would do it."],[2374.04,"Visiting unmarried women in their homes to bring them communion was not something men it was good for men to do."],[2384.26,"Uh there were, you know, all of these things were socially exercised."],[2388.18,"This wasn't good."],[2388.82,"And so you had specific women, usually older women, usually widows, uh, originally over the age of 60."],[2397.3,"They later lowered that to 40."],[2398.66,"That's the minimum age"],[2401.74,"But the and there were specific women who were given that job to help teach younger women, to help prepare women for baptism, to visit women in their homes privately."],[2418.02,"So on one hand, we're kind of in a similar situation today."],[2425.68,"Where there are a lot of problems in all churches, it's not just other churches, that result from accusations being made and things looking inappropriate, of male priests and male deacons meeting with women alone."],[2441.36,"There's all kinds of problems with that."],[2443.38,"So on the one hand, you have that, and so you have some bishops and stuff saying there might be a need to set aside certain women to help, at least help with some of this stuff, to prevent public scandal, to prevent, you know, these things, and to have to have this ministry."],[2463.22,"On the other side, you have people with an agenda who want women deacons, as in they want women to get ordained as deacons and do the liturgical deacon part in the service and go behind the altar and do all of that stuff."],[2480.47,"And obviously, none of our bishops are going to do that"],[2485.91,"So our bisho.ps aren't going to do that"],[2488.27,"But so that creates this awkward quasi political tension where the bishops are in this situation where it's like, well, okay, we can see the use for this, but how do we do it without it looking like something we don't want it to look like?"],[2507.95,"Without it being used, people trying to use it as some kind of stepping stone to try and do something else."],[2514.11,"How do we do it in a way that's appropriate, that actually fills this need of the church, but that isn't going to be seen as something else."],[2522.37,"Or as leading to something else."],[2524.61,"And so currently the Orthodox Church is sort of on the horns of this dilemma."],[2530.37,"And so because of that, nobody's really making deaconesses, even though, at least hypothetically, any Orthodox bishop could at any point make someone a deaconess, uh, if they wanted to."],[2544.57,"Um, so yeah, that's kind of where we are."],[2548.41,"But that's who St."],[2549.37,"Paul is talking about here."],[2552.62,"And this shouldn't be that controversial because we already saw Phoebe the deaconess."],[2557.02,"We've seen people referred to as women referred to as as deacons back in the book of Acts and at other places."],[2565.1,"But that's that's what he's talking about here."],[2567.98,"Um but since this is the New King James Version in our Orthodox Study Bible, uh, well, we know. who translated it"],[2577.06,"So does somebody have to be, does a man have to be married before he's oriented deacon?"],[2584.42,"Yes. Yeah, the practice, um the practice in the Orthodox Church is uh that uh deacons and priests get married before this is again a practical concern."],[2601.86,"You don't want clergy to have kind of a weird dual relationship in their congregation."],[2608.78,"Like with their single female congregants, you know."],[2611.42,"You know? Yeah, yeah, bachelor priests is not a good call."],[2617.78,"And I say this as someone who was a Protestant pastor while unmarried."],[2622.22,"I won't go into stories here, but the church is wise."],[2629.42,"In that regard."],[2630.78,"Did you have another question?"],[2633.18,"I did I forget."],[2634.3,"Okay. I feel like you'd have to manage to wrangle the bishop in to even do a marriage ceremony if a priest is involved in it anyway."],[2641.72,"Yeah. Yeah, there's yeah."],[2645.16,"Um so yeah St."],[2647.56,"Paul isn't directly addressing that here, but yeah, that's a one of those practical wisdom things."],[2657.32,"Okay. And then verse 12."],[2659.96,"Let deacons be the husbands of one wife, ruling their children in their own houses well."],[2665.16,"Bishops We already talked about the same thing."],[2668.64,"For those who have served well as deacons obtain for themselves a good standing and great boldness in the faith which is in Christ Jesus."],[2678.8,"Notice again those who have served well."],[2682.24,"This is like that desiring a good work."],[2687.42,"That actually has to actually be accomplished."],[2691.1,"So notice that St."],[2693.74,"Paul is not operating under some kind of clericalism here."],[2698.0,"Where somehow the fact that you've been made a bishop or the fact that you've been made a deacon gives you some kind of special standing with God."],[2706.61,"He says basically, yes, if you're a faithful deacon or bishop, that will give you good standing with God."],[2714.65,"But if you're an unfaithful one, you'd be better off never having that office."],[2721.13,"Yes, sir. Um absence of presbyters here."],[2725.85,"Yes. They'll pop up later in the cutting epistles."],[2730.81,"But it just seemed logical that there would be a section."],[2735.13,"So in the in these pastoral epistles, and you even see this throughout the end of the first century, when this transition is taking place."],[2746.21,"Where the terms episcopos and presbyter are used almost interchangeably sometimes."],[2753.57,"Um I say almost interchangeably because we never have a case where there's more than one episcopos in a city."],[2762.45,"Whereas there are plenty of cases where there's lots of presbyters in a given church."],[2767.26,"So it's not completely interchangeably."],[2769.74,"But in like Papius, for example, uh, who is a first century writer, uh, he refers to the apostles as presbyters sometimes."],[2781.71,"Uh and the idea there is that, and this is still true in the church, is that uh a person who is ordained to one office still holds the other offices."],[2792.35,"Like I'm technically still a deacon."],[2796.53,"I didn't give up the diaconal role to become a priest."],[2803.27,"And so a bishop is still a presbyter."],[2807.27,"Still a priest."],[2808.05,"Yeah, he still has all of that plus."],[2811.9,"So it's it's addition, not a lateral move."],[2816.91,"Um so that's that I think is is part of why, but also while the apostles are still around, you you have the apostolic office, which is its own thing."],[2833.3,"Once the apostolic office is gone, very quickly, by the end of the first century, in St."],[2838.5,"Ignatius's epistles, you have very clearly distinguished Episcopos, Presbyteros, Diaconos."],[2849.29,"You have the three offices very clear."],[2851.45,"But while the apostles are still there, the the the bishops are basically coming in, they don't become apostles, but they they they are the ones who occupy this successor to the apostles' role."],[2865.31,"So they are they are like substitutes for the apostles, the way presbyters will eventually be substitutes for bishops."],[2874.5,"And so while the apostles are still alive, they have kind of a different role than they will come to have once the apostles are all gone."],[2886.54,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2893.82,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2899.18,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":33.76,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: letters to individuals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>1 Timothy 3:1</b>; last time covered \"the completely <b>non-controversial</b> part\"</li><li class=\"d1\">this part \"less controversial and therefore perhaps less exciting\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ch. 3 slightly controversial, but \"I command women to remain silent\" <b>takes the cake</b> for 1 Timothy</li><li class=\"d0\">First of several letters directed to <b>individuals</b> rather than communities</li><li class=\"d1\">earlier ones: to a church (<b>Corinth</b>) or to the Christian communities of a region (<b>Galatia</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ephesians</b> = \"sort of a circular letter\" for the area around Ephesus</li><li class=\"d1\">this one written directly to <b>St. Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Already seen Paul's <b>father-son language</b> for his relationship to Timothy</li></ul>"},{"t":122.0,"lv":3,"h":"Pastoral epistles: mentorship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">1 Tim, 2 Tim, Titus = the <b>pastoral epistles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"pastoral\" → idea of <b>mentorship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>mentoring and preparing</b> Timothy for a <b>leadership role</b> in the churches</li><li class=\"d0\">Written very near end of Paul's life - likely <b>second Roman imprisonment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he knows he is <b>nearing the end</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Subtext of 1 &amp; 2 Tim: Paul has <b>traveled with and mentored</b> Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy = his <b>messenger and envoy</b>, sent when Paul couldn't go himself</li><li class=\"d1\">now <b>final instructions</b> - sending him out \"in Saint Paul's place\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul knows he's \"about to <b>depart</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":204.24,"lv":2,"h":"A generational transition","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Important for tonight: transition at <b>beginning of last third of 1st century</b></li><li class=\"d1\">~<b>66-67</b>: <b>Paul and Peter martyred</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some apostles martyred before; rest <b>except John</b> after - all within end of 1st c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Shift from the <b>apostles</b> - direct witnesses, disciples during Christ's earthly ministry - to <b>their disciples</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>first generation → second generation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why pastoral epistles \"<b>read a little differently</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some say Paul didn't write them, etc.</li><li class=\"d1\">part of the reason: a transition in the <b>type of NT literature</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul's epistles</b> among the first things written - Gospels came after</li><li class=\"d1\">written directly to communities about <b>practical things</b> going on there</li><li class=\"d1\">continuing his <b>pastoral role</b> in communities he founded</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans</b>: pastoral role in a church he didn't found, but had <b>recognized authority</b></li></ul>"},{"t":332.29,"lv":3,"h":"Why testimony gets written down","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">As apostles and eyewitnesses leave → need to <b>put testimony, wisdom, guidance in writing</b> for next generation</li><li class=\"d1\">\"they aren't going to be there in person to say that anymore\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers: why <b>St. Mark</b> writes his gospel</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter martyred; Mark to <b>Alexandria</b>, writes down what Peter told him</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter \"can't travel and preach\" → \"it has to be <b>written down</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Similar with <b>Matthew's gospel</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Luke</b>: \"a bunch of people have written things\" - pulled them together, talked to people</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John</b> seems familiar with the others (from John's gospel)</li><li class=\"d1\">deliberately <b>filling gaps</b> - maybe others we no longer have</li><li class=\"d1\">\"here's something else Christ said that I think is important\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same shift explains Paul: no longer writing to communities on <b>day-to-day</b> → writing to those who'll handle it in the <b>next generation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":449.67,"lv":2,"h":"Living memory and tradition","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Next generation has <b>living memory of the apostles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first generation: living memory of <b>Christ himself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">second: of the apostles</li><li class=\"d1\">third: \"sort of\" living memory</li><li class=\"d0\">By <b>end of 2nd century</b>: no living memory anymore</li><li class=\"d1\">fathers at end 2nd / beginning 3rd c.: <b>another transition</b>, \"outside living memory\"</li><li class=\"d1\">→ about <b>trusting what has come before</b></li><li class=\"d1\">develops into the whole process of <b>tradition</b> in the church</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul very much has this in mind writing to Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">preparing him for the role knowing he <b>won't be there \"in the flesh\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">true of 1 Timothy and later of <b>2 Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then: any questions? → pick up at <b>1 Tim 3:1</b></li></ul>"},{"t":543.49,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 3:1 - Bishop as overseer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.1: <b>\"This is a faithful saying\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First the <b>translation of \"bishop\"</b> - \"something people will have an issue with\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>episcopos</i> = pretty much always translated <b>bishop</b> in English</li><li class=\"d1\">could just leave it in Greek</li><li class=\"d0\">Literally <b>\"someone who watches over\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">often rendered <b>overseer</b> - \"super literal\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>scopos</i> as in <b>tele-scope, micro-scope</b> = to see, to look</li><li class=\"d0\">Behind the Greek word: a <b>Hebrew word from the OT</b></li></ul>"},{"t":600.22,"lv":3,"h":"The Hebrew shepherd behind it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew root = <b>to see, to look</b></li><li class=\"d1\">noun form = <b>herdsman or shepherd</b>, one who watches over flocks and herds</li><li class=\"d0\">An <b>adaptation</b> in Greek - Paul \"<b>not inventing</b> a new term\" or new office</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"here's how I think we should structure the church. Tell me what you think\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Leaders of Israel in OT = the <b>Shepherds of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">whether chewed out or praised; \"not always good shepherds\" but <b>to be shepherds</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Latin: <b>pastor</b> = shepherd - they knew the root behind <i>episcopos</i></li><li class=\"d1\">lost in English \"bishop\" - etymology? \"I do not know\" (Fr Andrew studying Old English)</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: is <i>episcopos</i> the normal Greek word for shepherd? → <b>no</b></li><li class=\"d1\">it's the word the <b>Greek OT</b> uses to translate the Hebrew for herdsman; other Greek words for shepherd</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul takes it \"very deliberately\" from the <b>Greek of the OT</b> for this role</li></ul>"},{"t":738.56,"lv":2,"h":"Same religion, same structures","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why it matters - \"as I harp on all the time\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Modern Western idea: Christ comes, Spirit comes → \"<b>starting from scratch, new religion time</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not how any apostle saw themselves - <b>same religion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now the <b>Messiah</b> has come (expected) and the <b>Holy Spirit</b> (Joel prophesied)</li><li class=\"d0\">Apostles structuring church for the future: <b>not inventing a new structure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not borrowing a \"<b>magisterial bishop</b> from Roman civil government\" (old Protestant seminary professor) - \"thinking like a governor\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Directly continuing: <i>episcopos</i>, <i>presbyter</i> = <b>elder</b> in OT</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>70 elders</b>, elders of Israel, <b>Sanhedrin</b> = presbyters in Greek</li><li class=\"d0\">All structures of Israel in OT remain as structures of <b>Israel in the NT = the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ fulfills them - not scrapped, replaced by something new → <b>\"fulfilled and developed\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Objection: Paul can't have written it - <b>no bishops yet</b>, \"starting from scratch\"</li><li class=\"d1\">answer: structures <b>already existed</b>, put into place in a Christian context</li><li class=\"d1\">Acts: <b>elders in Ephesus</b>; Paul didn't invent it - <b>Sanhedrin and elders in Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christian presbyters, elders, overseers, shepherds, pastors - \"not any kind of <b>radical departure</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":931.69,"lv":2,"h":"3:1-2: a good work, and the list","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.1: he who desires this <b>position of leadership</b> of a Christian community <b>desires a good work</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"a good desire no matter why they want it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doing it <b>faithfully</b> is the good work</li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier: <b>zealous of good work</b> - this is one way to do that</li><li class=\"d1\">must then be <b>about doing the good work</b> - \"not just about the authority\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.2: <b>blameless, husband of one wife, temperate, sober-minded, of good behavior, hospitable, able to teach</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why tell Timothy? He'll be <b>helping appoint bishops</b> over these churches</li><li class=\"d1\">\"these are the kind of people you need to put into this office\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: (catechumen) question about this - \"no one here has learned everything\" → we'll get to it in a second</li></ul>"},{"t":1038.7,"lv":3,"h":"Blameless; one-woman man","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Blameless</b> ≠ never sinned - \"otherwise we wouldn't have any bishops\"</li><li class=\"d1\">refers to <b>public guilt, shame, scandal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more like <b>good reputation</b> - not publicly seen to be problematic</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Husband of one wife</b> = literally <b>\"one-woman man\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not meant to imply he <b>has to be married</b></li><li class=\"d1\">means he <b>can't have been married more than once</b></li><li class=\"d1\">widowed then remarried, <b>divorced</b> and remarried, or <b>polygamous</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people unaware major Jewish communities practiced <b>polygamy until 10th c. AD</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul saying \"<b>none of that</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1138.28,"lv":3,"h":"Why bishops are unmarried today","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Recall the passage on <b>Peter's wife and kids</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Current practice tied to nature of the <b>episcopate</b> now</li><li class=\"d1\">early church: bishop = bishop of <b>a city</b>, presided at that one church</li><li class=\"d1\">growth: <b>Antioch</b> with 7-8 churches plus village churches → bishop sends <b>presbyters</b> to preside where he isn't</li><li class=\"d0\">Now <b>huge geographic areas</b></li><li class=\"d1\">old country: <b>hundreds of bishops in Russia</b> over little territories</li><li class=\"d1\">US: <b>Bishop Nicholas</b>, Miami, covers <b>North Carolina to Memphis to Lafayette</b></li><li class=\"d1\">before COVID <b>traveling every weekend</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not conducive to having a <b>wife and children</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So for last several centuries <b>generally</b> only unmarried men made bishops - \"tons of exceptions\"</li><li class=\"d1\">best source of candidates: <b>monastics</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1305.14,"lv":3,"h":"Not against marriage; Paul's reason","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not because something wrong with being married - \"be very clear\"</li><li class=\"d1\">common in same centuries for <b>widowed priests</b> to be consecrated bishops</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Bishop John</b> in our archdiocese: married with children, wife passed, kids adults with own households → consecrated</li><li class=\"d0\">So <b>not being married</b> = a <b>practical consideration</b></li><li class=\"d1\">based on Paul: \"don't I have the right to bring a <b>wife and children</b>, as St. Peter does?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul doesn't: <b>itinerant evangelist</b>, prison, shipwreck</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a practical thing and a question of <b>calling</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's emphasis here: married <b>only once - tops</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1397.41,"lv":2,"h":"3:2-5: ruling his own house","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Rest of v.2: temperate, sober-minded, good behavior, hospitable, <b>able to teach</b> - \"a big part of their role\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.3: <b>not given to wine</b> (\"we all know what that means\"), not violent, not greedy, <b>gentle</b>, not quarrelsome, not covetous</li><li class=\"d0\">vv.4-5: <b>rules his own house well</b>, children in submission with all reverence</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if a man does not know how to rule his own house, <b>how will he take care of the church of God?</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">OT: <b>priesthood, fatherhood, masculinity</b> connected at the most basic level</li><li class=\"d1\">\"what it is to be a man essentially is to be a <b>priest</b>\" within your household</li><li class=\"d0\">If married with children → <b>first priesthood is to his family</b></li><li class=\"d1\">represent <b>God to them and them to God</b>: prayer, leading in worship</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems in the house - not just a moral failing, he must be <b>paying attention</b></li><li class=\"d1\">children having problems → attend to that, \"not going and being a bishop\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If all is in order → can take on more in the <b>household of God</b>, the larger family of the community</li><li class=\"d0\">If not → <b>focus there</b>, \"not trying to take on more\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Having to choose where to devote <b>time and energy</b>; \"a lot of <b>priest families have suffered</b>\" - we don't heed Paul's advice</li></ul>"},{"t":1574.18,"lv":2,"h":"3:6-7: not a novice; outside testimony","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.6: <b>not a novice</b>, lest puffed up with pride he falls into <b>the same condemnation as the devil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Novice = <b>new</b> - didn't become a Christian last week</li><li class=\"d1\">\"seems smart and he talks good, so let's make him the bishop\" - \"not a good plan\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice <b>why</b>: not damage to the church, heresy, incorrect stuff</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not gonna be good <b>for him</b>\" - he'll get <b>proud</b>; \"remember how that worked out for the devil\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Another place of Paul's <b>mentorship</b> - concern for those in leadership</li><li class=\"d1\">on paper \"the best bishop ever\" - but bad for <b>his family</b> or <b>his salvation</b> → don't</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't matter how qualified\" or how much people like him</li><li class=\"d0\">v.7: <b>good testimony among those outside</b>, lest he fall into <b>reproach and the snare of the devil</b></li><li class=\"d1\">outside the Christian community - not just everybody inside thinks he's great</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul concerned about getting outsiders <b>to come inside</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wrong leader = <b>obstacle to the gospel</b> → not the right person</li></ul>"},{"t":1709.14,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: wanting to be bishop","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: story often told of someone who <b>did everything to avoid being made bishop</b> - yet v.1 says wanting it is good?</li><li class=\"d0\">He doesn't say wanting to be a bishop is good - says <b>being a bishop is a good work</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"an important distinction\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wanting the <b>authority or power</b> = not good</li><li class=\"d1\">wanting to <b>serve God and do the work</b> = good desire</li><li class=\"d0\">Right after, Paul gives a <b>checklist</b> of reasons to tell that person \"this isn't the right thing\" - \"maybe not, maybe not, maybe not\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Augustine</b>: \"literally drug him to the front of the church in tears\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they thought he cried from honor and spiritual experience; he: \"no, <b>I was terrified</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not always true: someone doesn't want it and is <b>right</b> - knows themselves</li><li class=\"d0\">Sometimes <b>perfect person</b> partly because they don't want power or authority</li><li class=\"d1\">\"both could go either way\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1840.03,"lv":2,"h":"3:8: deacons","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.8: deacons must be <b>reverent, not double-tongued, not given to much wine, not greedy for money</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>holding the mystery of the faith with a pure conscience</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Likewise\"</b> = same goes for deacons</li><li class=\"d0\">Deacons roughly parallel to <b>Levites</b> in OT (talked about back in Acts)</li><li class=\"d1\">priesthood, then the other Levites not in the priestly family</li><li class=\"d1\">assisted and did all the <b>other sub-ministries</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deacon</b> = from the word for <b>servant</b></li><li class=\"d1\">continuation of <b>household of God</b> idea - servants in God's household</li></ul>"},{"t":1901.48,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: what do deacons do?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Protestant picture = deacons handled <b>money, food, practical things</b>; ours are <b>liturgical assistants</b> - how did that transition happen?</li><li class=\"d0\">That's the liturgical role - but ministry should be <b>larger</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Deacons responsible throughout church history for <b>communion to the sick</b></li><li class=\"d1\">making sure <b>widows, orphans, people in need</b> are taken care of</li><li class=\"d1\">\"external pastoral role\" = <b>ministry of mercy</b> to broader community</li><li class=\"d0\">US: no lively <b>permanent diaconate</b> historically - \"to our detriment\"; bishops working on it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Presbyter</b> = internal ministry of the church; substitute for the bishop</li><li class=\"d0\">Technically <b>presbyters have no liturgical role</b>; bishops and deacons do</li><li class=\"d1\">priests do the parts whoever isn't there would do</li><li class=\"d1\">bishop + deacon present: \"priests just stand there\"; bishop \"throws us a bone... so we don't fall asleep\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Liturgy not the whole role of deacon - \"any more than doing the services\" is for the priest</li></ul>"},{"t":2077.08,"lv":2,"h":"3:10: tested first","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Likewise\" - reiterates some of the same for deacons</li><li class=\"d0\">v.10: <b>let these also first be tested</b>, then serve as deacons, <b>being blameless</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>blameless</b> = same word, but referring to <b>the testing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"oh, nice guy, seems a good kid, <b>cares about people</b>, let's make him a deacon\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same reasoning: wrong person → bad for community, <b>for the person and his family</b>, everybody involved</li><li class=\"d0\">\"So there's <b>testing first</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2131.53,"lv":2,"h":"3:11 - \"women,\" not \"wives\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Probably the actual <b>controversial</b> part of tonight\" - v.11 <b>\"translated completely wrong\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">italicized words, \"<b>their</b>\" and <b>\"wives\"</b> - not what it says</li><li class=\"d1\">says <b>\"likewise women\"</b>: reverent, not slanderers, temperate, faithful in all things</li><li class=\"d0\">It's about <b>deaconesses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">deaconess = a <b>separate office</b>, not women who are deacons</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul again says <b>\"likewise\"</b> - marks shift from one office to the next: \"likewise deacons, likewise women\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The word is <b>not the word for wife</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>gynē</i> = woman or wife (like <i>anēr</i> = man or husband; <i>anthropos</i> = mankind)</li><li class=\"d1\">word used here = a different form, <b>womanhood, womankind</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So not wives, and not just women who are deacons - \"it would have been the other word\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2263.89,"lv":3,"h":"Deacon women in church orders","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Early <b>church orders</b> (<b>Didache</b>, <b>Apostolic Constitutions</b>): no word \"deaconess\" - literally <b>\"deacon women\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"here's what deacons do. Here's what deacon women do\" - <b>different things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a different office\" = <b>ancient office of deaconess</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Still <b>technically exists</b> in the Orthodox Church - just not used much</li><li class=\"d1\">some <b>abbesses</b> of convents made deaconesses by the bishop</li><li class=\"d1\">otherwise \"fallen into disuse\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Some trepidation - \"controversial anyway\"; a lot of <b>dialogue</b> in the Orthodox Church right now</li></ul>"},{"t":2349.24,"lv":3,"h":"Why deaconesses existed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reason: things <b>culturally inappropriate for men</b> to do</li><li class=\"d1\">people baptized <b>naked</b> - male deacons not to go down to the water with a naked woman → women did it</li><li class=\"d1\">bringing <b>communion to unmarried women at home</b> - not good for men</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Specific women</b>, usually <b>older, usually widows</b></li><li class=\"d1\">originally over <b>60</b>; later lowered to <b>40</b> (minimum age)</li><li class=\"d0\">Their jobs</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>teach younger women</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>prepare women for baptism</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>visit women in their homes</b> privately</li></ul>"},{"t":2425.68,"lv":3,"h":"Today's dilemma","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Similar situation today - \"all churches, not just other churches\"</li><li class=\"d1\">problems from <b>accusations and things looking inappropriate</b> when male priests/deacons meet women alone</li><li class=\"d0\">One side: some bishops say <b>set aside certain women</b> to help</li><li class=\"d1\">prevent <b>public scandal</b>, have this ministry</li><li class=\"d0\">Other side: people \"with an <b>agenda</b>\" - want women <b>ordained as deacons</b> in the liturgical role, \"behind the altar\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"none of our bishops are gonna do that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Awkward <b>quasi-political tension</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how to do it without it being a <b>stepping stone</b> to something else</li><li class=\"d1\">fill the need appropriately without being seen as leading elsewhere</li><li class=\"d0\">Church \"on the <b>horns of this dilemma</b>\" → <b>nobody's making deaconesses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">though any Orthodox bishop <b>could at any point</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Shouldn't be that controversial: <b>Phoebe the deaconess</b>; women referred to as deacons in <b>Acts</b> and elsewhere</li><li class=\"d0\">\"That's what he's talking about here\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: Orthodox Study Bible = <b>New King James</b> - \"we know who translated it?\" (cut off)</li></ul>"},{"t":2577.06,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: married before ordained?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: does a man have to be <b>married before he's a deacon</b>? → yes</li><li class=\"d0\">Practice: <b>deacons and priests marry before</b> ordination - \"a practical concern\"</li><li class=\"d1\">avoid a <b>weird dual relationship</b> with single female congregants</li><li class=\"d1\">\"bachelor priest is not a good call\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he was a <b>Protestant pastor while unmarried</b> - \"won't go into stories\"; \"the church is wise\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: (another question forgotten)</li><li class=\"d1\">comment: you'd have to <b>wrangle the bishop</b> for a marriage ceremony if a priest is involved</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul not addressing it directly here - \"one of those <b>practical wisdom</b> things\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2657.32,"lv":2,"h":"3:12-13: those who serve well","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.12: <b>deacons husbands of one wife</b>, ruling children and own houses well - \"same thing\" as bishops</li><li class=\"d0\">v.13: those who <b>served well</b> obtain <b>good standing and great boldness in the faith in Christ Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Like \"desiring a good work\" - it <b>actually has to be accomplished</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul not operating under <b>clericalism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">being made bishop or deacon gives <b>no special standing with God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">faithful deacon or bishop → <b>good standing with God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unfaithful one → \"better off <b>never having that office</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2721.13,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: where are presbyters?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>absence of presbyters</b> here</li><li class=\"d0\">They'll <b>pop up later</b> in the epistles</li><li class=\"d0\">In pastoral epistles and through end of 1st c. (transition): <i>episcopos</i> and <i>presbyter</i> used <b>almost interchangeably</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"almost\": never more than <b>one episcopos in a city</b>; plenty of presbyters in a church</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Papias</b>, 1st-c. writer, sometimes calls the <b>apostles presbyters</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Still true: one ordained to an office <b>still holds the other offices</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm technically still a <b>deacon</b>\" - didn't give up diaconal role to become a priest</li><li class=\"d1\">a bishop is still a presbyter, \"all of that plus\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>addition, not a lateral move</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2816.91,"lv":3,"h":"Apostolic office, then three offices","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">While apostles are around: the <b>apostolic office</b>, \"its own thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Once gone - by end of 1st c., <b>Ignatius' epistles</b>: clearly distinguished <i>episcopos, presbyteros, diakonos</i></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>three offices</b> very clear</li><li class=\"d0\">Meanwhile bishops <b>don't become apostles</b> but occupy the <b>successor to the apostles</b> role</li><li class=\"d1\">\"substitutes for the apostles\" - as presbyters will eventually be substitutes for bishops</li><li class=\"d0\">While apostles alive, bishops have <b>a different role</b> than they will once the apostles are done</li></ul>"}]});
